(go/type Prog "Prog describes a single machine instruction.\n\nThe general instruction form is:\n\n\t(1) As.Scond From [, ...RestArgs], To\n\t(2) As.Scond From, Reg [, ...RestArgs], To, RegTo2\n\nwhere As is an opcode and the others are arguments:\nFrom, Reg are sources, and To, RegTo2 are destinations.\nRestArgs can hold additional sources and destinations.\nUsually, not all arguments are present.\nFor example, MOVL R1, R2 encodes using only As=MOVL, From=R1, To=R2.\nThe Scond field holds additional condition bits for systems (like arm)\nthat have generalized conditional execution.\n(2) form is present for compatibility with older code,\nto avoid too much changes in a single swing.\n(1) scheme is enough to express any kind of operand combination.\n\nJump instructions use the To.Val field to point to the target *Prog,\nwhich must be in the same linked list as the jump instruction.\n\nThe Progs for a given function are arranged in a list linked through the Link field.\n\nEach Prog is charged to a specific source line in the debug information,\nspecified by Pos.Line().\nEvery Prog has a Ctxt field that defines its context.\nFor performance reasons, Progs are usually bulk allocated, cached, and reused;\nthose bulk allocators should always be used, rather than new(Prog).\n\nThe other fields not yet mentioned are for use by the back ends and should\nbe left zeroed by creators of Prog lists.\n" (struct
    ^{:line 305 :tag (* Link)} Ctxt ; linker context
    ^{:line 306 :tag (* Prog)} Link ; next Prog in linked list
    ^{:line 307 :tag Addr} From ; first source operand
    ^{:line 308 :tag (slice AddrPos)} RestArgs ; can pack any operands that not fit into {Prog.From, Prog.To}, same kinds of operands are saved in order
    ^{:line 309 :tag Addr} To ; destination operand (second is RegTo2 below)
    ^{:line 310 :tag (* Prog)} Pool ; constant pool entry, for arm,arm64 back ends
    ^{:line 311 :tag (* Prog)} Forwd ; for x86 back end
    ^{:line 312 :tag (* Prog)} Rel ; for x86, arm back ends
    ^{:line 313 :tag int64} Pc ; for back ends or assembler: virtual or actual program counter, depending on phase
    ^{:line 314 :tag src/XPos} Pos ; source position of this instruction
    ^{:line 315 :tag int32} Spadj ; effect of instruction on stack pointer (increment or decrement amount)
    ^{:line 316 :tag As} As ; assembler opcode
    ^{:line 317 :tag int16} Reg ; 2nd source operand
    ^{:line 318 :tag int16} RegTo2 ; 2nd destination operand
    ^{:line 319 :tag uint16} Mark ; bitmask of arch-specific items
    ^{:line 320 :tag uint16} Optab ; arch-specific opcode index
    ^{:line 321 :tag uint8} Scond ; bits that describe instruction suffixes (e.g. ARM conditions, RISCV Rounding Mode)
    ^{:line 322 :tag uint8} Back ; for x86 back end: backwards branch state
    ^{:line 323 :tag uint8} Ft ; for x86 back end: type index of Prog.From
    ^{:line 324 :tag uint8} Tt ; for x86 back end: type index of Prog.To
    ^{:line 325 :tag uint8} Isize)) ; for x86 back end: size of the instruction in bytes



